      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "932605d9-a5c0-531c-97c6-24ef3204b9b6",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Roy Orbison, Cyndi Lauper\n- Products: Early Orbison, \"I Drove All Night\", \"Pretty One\", \"Uptown\"\n- Companies: Monument Records\n- Places: Hendersonville, Tennessee\n- Topics: compilation albums, posthumous releases\n\nFacts:\n- Roy Kelton Orbison was born on April 23, 1936, and he became an American singer-songwriter known for his distinctive, impassioned voice, complex song structures, and dark emotional ballads.\n- Early Orbison was recorded by Roy Orbison on the Monument Records label at Monument's studios in Hendersonville, Tennessee, and the album was released in 1964.\n- Early Orbison was essentially a compilation of songs from Roy Orbison's first two Monument albums.\n- Early Orbison is notable for including \"Pretty One,\" which was the B-side of Roy Orbison's second Monument single, \"Uptown.\"\n- Roy Orbison recorded \"I Drove All Night\" in 1987, and the song had originally been intended for him.\n- Cyndi Lauper's version of \"I Drove All Night\" became a top 10 hit on both sides of the Atlantic in 1989.\n- Roy Orbison died on December 6, 1988.\n- Roy Orbison's rendition of \"I Drove All Night\" was released in 1992, after Cyndi Lauper's version had become a hit.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "8599f3f2-20b3-5017-8bef-006c3f68556e",
        "text": "Page: Early Orbison. Sentence: Early Orbison is an album recorded by Roy Orbison on the Monument Records label at their studios in Hendersonville, Tennessee and released in 1964.\nPage: Early Orbison. Sentence: Essentially a compilation of songs from his first two Monument albums, it is most noteworthy for containing \"Pretty One\", the \"B\" side of Orbison's second Monument single, \"Uptown\".\nPage: Roy Orbison. Sentence: Roy Kelton Orbison (April 23, 1936 – December 6, 1988) was an American singer-songwriter known for his distinctive, impassioned voice, complex song structures, and dark emotional ballads.\nPage: I Drove All Night. Sentence: The song was originally intended for Roy Orbison, who recorded it in 1987, the year before his death, but his rendition was not released until 1992, after Lauper's version of it had become a top 10 hit on both sides of the Atlantic in 1989.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
